        "---\n",
        "title: \"Knots to Narnia\"\n",
        "subtitle: \"A homage to William Thurston using modern computer visualization\"\n",
        "author: \"Luca Leon Happel\"\n",
        "date: \"2026-03-18\"\n",
        "categories:\n",
        "  - math\n",
        "  - topology\n",
        "  - knot theory\n",
        "  - visualization\n",
        "  - English\n",
        "format:\n",
        "  html:\n",
        "    code-fold: true\n",
        "draft: true\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "The Fields Medalist William Thurston is famous for revolutionizing low-dimensional topology and geometry, especially through his work on 3-manifolds and the geometrization conjecture. He is beloved not just by me but by the general mathematical community for his playful, story-driven approach to algebraic topology. \n",
        "In \"Knots to Narnia\", he imagines a magic portal shaped like a loop or knot that splits space into \"parallel worlds\". An ordinary loop produces just two worlds, while a trefoil knot gives several distinct worlds connected by precise rules. By tracking simple moves through the knot, Thurston shows how these rules encode a branched covering structure in an unusually vivid, accessible way.\n",
        "\n",
        "In this post, I will present a modern visualization of Thurston's \"Knots to Narnia\" concept using computer graphics. We will explore how different knots can create various \"parallel worlds\" and how these worlds are interconnected.\n",
        "\n",
        "## First example: The Unknot\n",
        "\n",
        "Let us look at the unknot embedded in $\\mathbb{R}^3$:\n",
        "\n",
        "We render the scene using a custom **ray marcher** built from scratch.\n",
        "Unlike a traditional raytracer that solves ray-surface intersections analytically,\n",
        "ray marching steps along each ray incrementally—making it straightforward to\n",
        "later replace Euclidean straight-line steps with geodesic steps in a curved geometry.\n",
        "\n",
        "The core loop is:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. For each pixel, cast a ray from the camera.\n",
        "2. **Step the ray** according to a `step_ray` function (currently Euclidean: $\\mathbf{p} \\mapsto \\mathbf{p} + dt \\cdot \\mathbf{d}$).\n",
        "3. At each step, evaluate a signed distance function (SDF) to check proximity to the surface.\n",
        "4. On hit, compute Phong lighting; otherwise, return the background."
